/**
@page tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_running Use ExaGRyPE

The build and running of ExaGRyPE is wrapped by a Python API for convenience. By calling
the Python script in a command terminal, one can easily generate an executable that can run on any
computational platform, from a student's laptop to supercomputer clusters. Different scenarios can be
picked by feeding different physics parameters into the Python script; the simulation setup and running parameters
can also be controlled directly via the command line.

Users are referred to the general \ref page_exahype2_tutorials of ExaHyPE 2 if they are interested in
what is going on behind the screens. We also provide a dedicated page 
for @ref tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_setup "setup of ExaGRyPE".

# Gauge Wave

The gauge wave scenario represents a dynamic foliation of a static Minkowski spacetime; it serves as
a good benchmark setup for users (and also developers) to test their implementations of the code. The detailed
explanations and equations of this benchmark can be found in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0305023">this paper</a>, section IV.B.
To run a gauge wave simulation in ExaGRyPE, use the command

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 ccz4.py -impl fd4-rk1-dsl -s gauge -maxh 0.05 -minh 0.05 -ps 9 -plt 0.1 -et 0.5 -exn gauge --domain_r 0.5 --ReSwi 0 -cfl 0.1 --KOSigma 8.0
export OMP_NUM_THREADS=1
mpirun -n 1 ./peano4_gauge
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Typing parameter --help makes the Python script yield further information and an explanation of each parameter option.
We recommend users not to change the running parameters of ExaGRyPE during benchmarking, as the parameter space is vast
and some combinations lead to unpredictable behaviour.

This example should yield a harmonic gauge wave propagating stably along the positive x-direction.

# Single Schwarzschild Black Hole

This scenario describes a single stable, non-spinning black hole in spacetime, located at the center of the
domain. One should expect the system to become (quasi-) static after some elapsed time. The puncture method is adopted here to
avoid domain excision, but at the cost of a high-resolution requirement.

The following example is computationally expensive and thus not suitable for a normal PC:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 ccz4.py -ext adm -impl fd4-rk1-dsl -s single-puncture -maxh 0.4 -minh 0.04 -ps 6 -plt 0.5 -et 100 -exn sbh --domain_r 9.0 --ReSwi 7 -cfl 0.1 -outdir /path/to/data/folder/ --KOSigma 8.0 -sommerfeld -so -interp second_order -restrict second_order
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This example should yield a stable and static black hole staying in the origin of the domain.

# Binary Black Hole Merger

Two black holes rotate around each other in a quasi-circular orbit, and finally merge at the center in this scenario.
We set the initial physics conditions of the system following <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0307027">this paper</a>, Table I.
Again, the following example is not suitable for a normal PC:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 ccz4.py -impl fd4-rk1-dsl -s two-punctures -maxh 0.4 -minh 0.04 -ps 8 -plt 0.5 -et 120 -exn bbh --domain_r 12.0 --ReSwi 6 -cfl 0.1 -outdir /path/to/data/folder/ --KOSigma 8.0 --BBHType 2 -sommerfeld -so -ext adm -interp second_order -restrict second_order
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This example should yield two black holes rotating and finally merging at the center.

# Clean up old and temporary files

The following statements remove all the glue code and auto-generated C++ files. This would be useful when you
want to make sure no legacy code is around. There is no risk in deleting them - if you rerun the Python script,
they will all be re-created.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm *.o *.cmake
rm README-*.md
rm Makefile CMakeLists.txt
rm Abstract*
rm -rf celldata facedata globaldata observers tasks vertexdata repositories
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

To get rid of the output files, type

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm *patch-file *vtu *.pvd *.bak *.csv
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

# Visualization

See \ref tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_visualize

Please notice the spatial filter is enabled by default, so the output of the examples above would be a slice of the x-y plane in the domain, instead of a complete cube.

*/
